title: 18 July 1974 — Meeting Sisco
new-page: true
view-if: {! return AnatolianRules.cyprusAtilla1.briefingScene(Q) === "cyprus_briefing_18"; !}

= 18 July 1974 — Meeting Sisco

Britain’s refusal had left Ecevit without the cooperation he had sought in London. The diplomatic effort now turned to Joseph Sisco, who hoped to persuade Turkey to refrain from intervening. For Ankara, however, another appeal for patience would not be enough; the government had to explain what concrete changes it required, both to its allies and to the political leaders at home.

Immediately after Ecevit repeated what he had said to Wilson and Callaghan, Sisco took the floor and began speaking quite forcefully to dissuade Turkey. He had also brought a message from Kissinger to the same effect:

Sisco: "America opposes a Turkish intervention because of the coup. We think this could lead to war between two NATO members."

After stating that Turkey had no intention of going to war with Greece, Ecevit tried to dispel the American administration's concerns. Beginning with the decision on poppy cultivation, he discussed Turkish-American relations and the periods through which they had passed. He even said that friendships could endure as long as they rested on sound and equal foundations. He said that even the Sixth Fleet could now visit Turkish ports more safely, because relations rested on a healthy foundation:

Ecevit: "...But it is impossible for Turkey to turn a blind eye to a fait accompli in Cyprus."

Sisco: "But you are not considering Greece's reaction. They will not leave this unanswered. War will break out and the region will be thrown into turmoil. You should know that your relations with the United States will also deteriorate."

Ecevit: "That is for them to decide."

The end of the meeting with Sisco, which had been interrupted after lunch and resumed for half an hour in the afternoon, was very interesting. Sisco, known as Kissinger's closest associate, had spoken with Callaghan and returned to Ecevit. He persisted in his position and seemed to have realized that the danger of intervention was much greater than he had estimated. According to those who witnessed it, the following exchange took place:

Sisco: "Mr. Ecevit, I am going from here to Athens. Give me your conditions in writing. We shall take them to the Greeks. I shall then return to Ankara and inform you of their answers."

Ecevit dictated them one by one:

Ecevit: "Our conditions are quite clear. To restore the balance and secure the Turkish community's safety, the presence of Turkish troops on the island must be accepted. Sampson must be replaced immediately, the Greek officers withdrawn, the Turkish community granted an area with an outlet to the sea, and a system established to control entry to and exit from the island. In other words, Turkey must be granted the opportunities the Greeks currently possess."

In a speech many described as "clear and resolute", Deniz Baykal explained the Ecevit government's "crisis diplomacy" (Realpolitik) and made it clear to the leaders that intervention was approaching.

Baykal: "How does the government view events? In our view, the London and Zurich agreements had lost their validity even before the coup. They continued in practice. The Treaty of Guarantee was also losing its effectiveness, and Turkey was unable to protect its rights arising from the agreements. This is the underlying cause of the latest events. We must now do more than explain our rights; we must take matters further. We shall seek our rights on the basis of an independent Cyprus. Our first objective is to ensure that the Turkish community obtains at least one port. There is a movement in the world to bring Makarios back. But that is not our objective. If we act, paths beyond London and Zurich may open before us. We do not regard the latest developments as a simple coup..."

